# E. HOST IDENTITY, LINEAGE, AND SECURITY-STATE CONTINUITY

## PM-HOST-001 — Host Identity Authority

Prime owns Host identity. Host identity is not merely a clonable disk UUID.

## PM-HOST-002 — Identity Assurance

Host identity declares an assurance/strength class such as hardware/enrollment anchored, externally enrolled, storage-only, or ambiguous. Remote/fleet mutation may require a minimum assurance class.

## PM-HOST-003 — Host Identity Lifecycle

Portable states include:

- `ENROLLING`;
- `ACTIVE`;
- `REBIND_REQUIRED`;
- `AMBIGUOUS_OR_FORKED`;
- `RETIRED`.

## PM-HOST-004 — Material Hardware Change

Materially different hardware normally produces a new Host identity plus an immutable lineage edge. Preserving the same Host identity requires an explicit mechanically proven continuity policy.

## PM-HOST-005 — Clone/Fork Safety

Cloned/forked storage cannot silently create two identical authoritative Hosts. Ambiguous/forked identity causes remote/fleet mutation to fail closed.

## PM-HOST-006 — Lineage Is Not Authority Inheritance

Lineage does not automatically transfer grants, remote trust, pending actions/transactions, support claims, disclosure contexts, or current-state cache.

## PM-HOST-007 — Manager State Scoping

Pure layout may be global. Principal preferences are principal-scoped. Host-derived cache/search/selection binds Host identity, lineage, principal, and relevant source/security epochs. Disclosure-gated detail is memory-only unless classification permits persistence.

## PM-HOST-008 — Host Rebind Discontinuity

If Host identity/lineage changes while Manager is connected, target-bound preflights, approvals, grants, projections, caches, and remote trust assumptions are invalidated/reset before further privileged operation.

## PM-SECSTATE-001 — Rollback-Sensitive Security State

Replay-sensitive security state cannot rely solely on restorable Host storage.

## PM-SECSTATE-002 — Security-State Anchor

Prime uses a rollback-resistant anchor class such as hardware monotonic state, external enrollment ledger, or another approved non-restorable trust anchor.

## PM-SECSTATE-003 — Security-State Version

Conceptually binds:

```text
host_identity
anchor_identity
security_epoch
monotonic_commit_index
authenticated_state_root
```

A coarse epoch alone is insufficient.

## PM-SECSTATE-004 — Crash-Consistent Security Commit

Rollback-sensitive state becomes authoritative only when durable state and the rollback-protected anchor consistently commit. Crash/mismatch recovers to the exact committed version or `SECURITY_STATE_UNCERTAIN`.

## PM-SECSTATE-005 — SecurityStateContinuityAssessment

Possible results include `CONTINUOUS`, `ROLLBACK_DETECTED`, and `UNVERIFIABLE`. Each high-risk operation declares the minimum continuity required.

## PM-SECSTATE-006 — Security-State History Discontinuity

Rollback detection creates an explicit trust/history discontinuity. Prime does not fabricate seamless evidence/audit continuity across a rewind.

## PM-SECSTATE-007 — Generation-Independent Readability

Minimum security continuity metadata/anchor semantics are readable by retained Recovery/security authority. An older Core that cannot interpret them fails affected high-risk operations closed.

