# H. MANAGEMENT CONTROL SESSIONS, DEPENDENCIES, AND CONTROL PATHS

## PM-CTRLSESS-001 — ManagementControlSession Registry

Every Prime-managed privileged control client must acquire an active ManagementControlSession lease before obtaining control authority.

## PM-CTRLSESS-002 — Session Scope

Can include Manager, Terminal, approved remote Manager, Oracle, or another explicitly admitted control client.

## PM-CTRLSESS-003 — Session Lifecycle

Portable states: `ESTABLISHING`, `ACTIVE`, `CLOSING`, `ENDED`, `LOST_OR_UNKNOWN`.

## PM-CTRLSESS-004 — Session Data

Contains client/system actor, principal where relevant, transport/path resources, source, lifecycle, revision, and disclosure classification.

## PM-CTRLSESS-005 — Continuity

Identity survives Core/transport restart only when mechanically proven; otherwise a new control-session identity is created.

## PM-DEP-001 — DependencyGraph

Edges include subject, dependency, type, direction, authority/source, revision, and closure state. Graph may be cyclic or incomplete. Missing edge does not prove missing dependency.

## PM-DEP-002 — Bounded Dependency Evaluation

Dependency analysis is work/cardinality bounded. Exceeding limits produces incomplete/UNKNOWN, never safe-by-timeout.

## PM-CTRLPATH-001 — ControlPathAssessment

Canonical scope covers every Prime-managed authorized active control session potentially affected by the effect, not only the requester.

## PM-CTRLPATH-002 — ControlPath Coverage

Assessment distinguishes complete coverage of Prime-managed control paths from partial/unknown external or unmanaged coverage.

## PM-CTRLPATH-003 — Disclosure-Safe Projection

Canonical impact may know another protected admin session exists while the requester receives only a safe reason such as “current management connectivity would be disrupted.”

